Install the kerbside-proxy wheel into kerbside images.

The kerbside-proxy transition replaced the in-tree Python SPICE proxy
with a compiled Rust binary supervised by "kerbside daemon run". That
binary is not part of the kerbside Python source tree installed by
/kerbside above; it ships in a separate maturin "bin" wheel (name
kerbside-proxy) that lays the compiled binary onto PATH.

Prefer a wheel staged into the build tree at kerbside/proxy-wheels/:
CI (and local development) stages one built from the checked-out
kerbside source, so pull requests test the proxy in lockstep with the
Python side. When no wheel is staged -- every production image build --
fall back to installing kerbside-proxy from PyPI.

The fallback is now required rather than merely safe: the Rust proxy
merged to kerbside's default branch (shakenfist/kerbside#99) and
kerbside-proxy 0.3.0 is published on PyPI, so a kerbside container
without the binary fails at startup. The PyPI install is deliberately
unpinned, matching the unpinned clone of kerbside itself in
assemble-source.sh: both track their respective tips, and the SPICE
console CI lane guards against contract drift between them. It also
bypasses upper-constraints (the false argument), which do not know
about kerbside-proxy.

